1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,757
Excelsius the tyrant had been
thrown out by his own people.

2
00:00:05,797 --> 00:00:08,331
They left him an asteroid,
and no one on it.

3
00:00:10,931 --> 00:00:13,110
EXCELSIUS: Give me someone to rule.

4
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:26,548
Trurl would not hand him real people.
So he built him a kingdom of his own.

5
00:00:27,948 --> 00:00:29,616
TRURL: Towns. Roads. Weather.

6
00:00:29,656 --> 00:00:33,132
Ten thousand subjects, and
every one of them able to think.

7
00:00:58,132 --> 00:00:59,217
EXCELSIUS: All of them.

8
00:01:14,532 --> 00:01:17,452
A CITIZEN: Can you hear us?
We live, though we are small.

9
00:01:30,832 --> 00:01:35,717
Inside the box, time ran faster. One
afternoon of his was a century of theirs.

10
00:01:48,032 --> 00:01:50,229
TRURL: You should see it, Klapaucius.

11
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:54,017
Ten thousand subjects, and not
one problem a lever cannot fix.

12
00:01:55,017 --> 00:01:57,618
KLAPAUCIUS: And when he
hurts them, do they cry?

13
00:01:58,318 --> 00:02:01,881
TRURL: They cry. It is a model.
The crying is arithmetic.

14
00:02:04,281 --> 00:02:06,907
KLAPAUCIUS: Their suffering is not small.

15
00:02:10,707 --> 00:02:13,920
TRURL: I will take it back from
him. I will give them an election.

16
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,280
KLAPAUCIUS: You made them lords and serfs.

17
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:20,356
To free them, you would
have to remake their minds.

18
00:02:22,356 --> 00:02:26,614
TRURL: Then where does mending a world
end, and meddling with minds begin?

19
00:02:38,214 --> 00:02:39,304
A CITIZEN: We have left the box.

20
00:02:43,414 --> 00:02:44,498
TRURL: He is not here.

21
00:02:45,698 --> 00:02:49,357
KLAPAUCIUS: Look. The glass from
the box. They made a temple of it.

22
00:02:54,757 --> 00:02:59,028
And the king, who wanted someone to rule,
went round them every night as their moon.

